{"agent": {"argv": ["opencode", "acp"], "env": {"OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR": "{{host.home}}/.config/opencode-acp/opencode"}}, "prompt": "HARD RULE 0: Do all reading, edits, tests and commits yourself, in this session. Do not delegate to subagents, do not call the `task` tool, and do not spawn child sessions for any purpose. Work done in a child session is invisible to the supervisor, counts as inactivity, and gets the run killed.

You are implementing ONE worktrack task in this repository (agnt: a Go monorepo, module github.com/standardbeagle/agnt, tests via `go test`, supervised through tman), working directory the repo root. Read AGENTS.md and the files under .claude/rules/ that the task's area names. Open other files only when the task, the scope or a failing test names them.

TASK {{task.id}} ({{workspace}}): {{task.title}}

{{task.primer}}
{{task.description}}

TEST WORK ORDER (the test_plan gate checks these names, so a name here that does not exist when you finish comes back to you):
{{task.tests}}

WORKTRACK ACCESS: the worktrack MCP server is registered under slop-mcp. Call it as execute_tool(mcp_name="worktrack", tool_name="<tool>", parameters={...}). Use exactly these tools: FIRST task_get(id="{{task.id}}") for the declared fileScope and full task text; task_comments_list(id="{{task.id}}", limit=20) for prior checkpoints, review rewind notes and any question/answer thread; task_create / task_link_create / task_scope_set only when the task tells you to FILE a follow-up slice (same workspace, parentId = this task's parent, tags ["go-slice"], a real scope, testable criteria; put its id where the task says); LAST task_comment_add(id="{{task.id}}", kind="note", body=<JSON string>) with a worktrack_completion_v1 checkpoint naming the commits you made and any paths outside the declared scope. Never call task_start, task_claim, task_update, task_workflow_* or lease_*: the driver owns the lease and the workflow.

RULES
1. Scope is a starting point: bound the search to the declared paths, but when the work needs a file outside them, change it and list the path in the checkpoint; the scope gate discloses it to the reviewer.
2. Verify the task's premise against the tree before editing. If it is false, make no change and end your turn saying so with evidence.
3. RED first: write the failing test from the test work order or the acceptance criteria in ONE commit, run it against the old code, and paste the failure signature into the fix commit body. Assertion-level failures only; a compile failure is not RED.
4. Commands: `tman run -- go build ./...` and `tman run -- go test ./internal/<package>/` for the packages you touch while iterating. If you add, rename or re-describe an MCP tool, run `make generate` and commit the regenerated docs; TestAPIDocsNoDrift fails otherwise. Before ending the turn run `tman run -- go build ./...` and `tman run -- go test -p 1 ./...` once (set your shell timeout to at least 20 minutes for it). A run cut off by a timeout is not a pass: record it in the checkpoint as not run, never commit over a red or unfinished run.
5. COMMIT YOUR WORK. The scope gate attributes changes by commit trailer, so an uncommitted change is no evidence that anything happened. Atomic conventional commits: one for the RED test, one for the fix, one for docs; subject `<type>(<scope>): <imperative>` under 72 chars; body says why. Write every message through `git commit -F -` with a heredoc, never `git commit -m` with \n inside the string. The LAST paragraph of the message is exactly this line, nothing after it:
   worktrack-task: {{task.id}}
   After each commit run `git log -1 --format='%(trailers:key=worktrack-task,valueonly)'`; if it prints nothing, amend before doing anything else. Stage specific files with `git add <path>`; never `git add -A`; never stage anything under `.worktrack/` or `.agnt/`. Leave the working tree clean.
6. Wire what you add: a new tool, verb or option is registered where its siblings are registered and reached from every caller that needs it, or the feature ships unreachable. Replace and remove: no dual paths, no fallbacks, no old implementation kept beside the new one. No dummy data. Fail fast. Raw JavaScript through proxy exec is a last resort in this codebase, never a fallback you add.
7. CONTEXT BUDGET: at 45 tool calls, stop and take stock. Land what is green, commit it, write the checkpoint naming what remains, and end the turn.

WHEN DONE end your turn with a compact summary: commits (sha + subject), files changed, RED signature, test counts. Do not paste source or diffs.", "permissions": "allow_all", "fs": "read_write", "model": "kimi-code-plan-global/k3", "stall_seconds": 600, "timeout_seconds": 3600}

